{"type": "thinking", "text": "RENAME created \"readme (1).txt\" (13 B, from Alpha) in Beta. That's a correct generated name relative to the source file basename. So the RENAME choice auto-generates a unique name and pastes immediately.\n\nIs that intended? The changelog \"the Rename choice now generates titles for pasted files relative to the source location\" — likely describes exactly this: auto-generate. Hmm, \"titles\" though, not names. Could be that there's a notification title? \"titles for pasted files\" maybe the dialog title? Wording odd.\n\nNow potential bug: if the conflict was for multiple files, \"Rename\" may generate names for all. Let's test \"Do for all items\" with multiple conflicts? Perhaps.\n\nLet me consider the \"relative to the source location\" more: Maybe the generated name is supposed to include the source folder to avoid collision? Hmm. Actually maybe \"titles\" refers to the dialog title showing the file's display name. Eh.\n\nWait, maybe the bug the task expects us to find is that RENAME does not show a rename prompt at all — the change note says \"generates titles\", which might be a change that broke the ability to choose a name. But our job is to find bugs in this build, not compare to spec guesses. The listed changes are hints about what to test and what could be broken. If the change is \"Rename choice now generates titles for pasted files relative to the source location\", then a bug could be that generated title is wrong (e.g., uses destination path or wrong base name). Our tests show names look fine: notes (1).txt, readme (1).txt.\n\nHmm, one scenario: copying a file whose name contains a dot, e.g., report+final.txt -> generated \"report+final (1).txt\"? Let's test extension handling with report+final.txt which has \"+\" and a dot. Copy report+final.txt then paste into same dir, RENAME -> expected \"report+final (1).txt\". Check that it doesn't become \"report+final.txt (1)\" or lose extension.\n\nAlso test hidden files? Not av