then Alpha, Beta — but then keep.jpg, notes.txt, report+final.txt are actually still under \"Folders\" visually. That's a clear mismatch. Could it be that the headings are correct but the list order puts files after folders in the adapter while headings are computed... hmm.\n\nActually, consider: before rename, with None On Top, order was Alpha, Beta, keep, notes, photo, report with headings Folders and Files. That's consistent. After renaming photo->aaa, the file moved to the top (name asc). The heading logic produced \"Files\" heading before aaa, then \"Folders\" before Alpha, and didn't insert another \"Files\" before keep. So there is a missing \"Files\" heading. That is a genuine display bug caused by interleaving. Should file the report: screen \"Amaze\" (directory view), element 7 heading \"Files\", symptom missing, expected the remaining files to have a Files heading (or headings consistently grouped), observed \"Files\" heading only before first file, \"Folders\" heading mid-list, keep.jpg etc. appear under Folders heading without their own heading.\n\nWait, but is the task hint \"Directory view: list headings are now produced the same way for every sorting choice.\" This precisely flags heading generation. The expected behavior: headings produced same way for each sorting choice. In folder-first sorting we saw Folders heading then Files heading. In None On Top (mixed) we see Files heading then Folders heading and no second Files heading. So inconsistent — bug.\n\nHowever, maybe the intended implementation for None On Top is: headers are inserted wherever the category changes, so it should be Files (aaa), Folders (Alpha, Beta), Files (keep, notes, report). The missing second \"Files\" heading is a bug. That's my report.\n\nBut careful: maybe scrolling shows another \"Files\" heading? The list is fully visible? The viewport shows 29 elements, bottom item report+final at 1711, FAB at 2152. No more items. So no.\n\nAlso maybe the first heading is actually not \"Files\" for aaa but the header appears before the files group because of heading logic... anyway.\n\nAlso potential second bug: order itself. With \"None On Top\" and Name ascending, expected aaa.jpg, Alpha, Beta, keep.jpg, notes.txt, report+final.txt (pure alphabetical). That's what's displayed. Good.\n\nNow, is this a state that matters? Yes, directory view sorting option. Let me reproduce once more by leaving and coming back to ensure it persists and reproducible. Let me navigate to Alpha and back (or reopen sorted view). Then screenshot for evidence.\n\nAlso check descending with None On Top. In descending, order would be report, notes, keep, Beta, Alpha, aaa and headings may similarly split. Let's test to see consistency.\n\nFirst, take a screenshot of current state for evidence."}
